\begin{abstract}
The First Atoms Engine (FAE) is a conceptual propulsion architecture based on
engineered atomic energy-density gradients. Instead of generating thrust via
reaction mass or spacetime curvature, FAE induces motion as drift through
stability-biased atomic fields. This paper defines the atomic field framework,
system architecture, and effective motion law of FAE, and positions it as a
relativistic but non-superluminal propulsion concept.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conventional propulsion systems rely on macroscopic forces: chemical thrust,
electric drives, or speculative spacetime manipulation. The First Atoms Engine
(FAE) proposes a different regime: motion emerging from atomic energy-density
gradients. By constructing an internal atomic anchor and coupling it to an
externally defined stability field, FAE defines a gradient that a craft can
drift through without classical exhaust.

\section{Atomic Stability Fields}
FAE constructs two atomic-scale fields:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,ext} = f(\text{cosmic telemetry}, \text{spectral density}, Z_{env})
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,int} = g(\text{Pt/U fusion anchor}, \text{MHD confinement}, \Psi_{core})
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_{A,ext}$ is an external atomic stability field derived from
environmental and astrophysical inputs, and $\Phi_{A,int}$ is an internal
anchor generated by controlled nuclear states.

The atomic stability gradient is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A} = \Phi_{A,ext} - \Phi_{A,int}
\end{equation}
This gradient sets the direction and magnitude of potential drift.

\section{System Architecture}
The FAE architecture consists of the following conceptual modules:

\subsection{Atomic State Controller}
An atomic state controller modulates energy levels using electromagnetic and
nuclear field shaping. It defines high-energy and low-energy atomic regions,
forming the basis for $\Phi_{A,ext}$ and $\Phi_{A,int}$.

\subsection{Fusion Anchor Module}
A Pt/U fusion anchor provides a coherent, low-entropy core:
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_{core} = h(\text{Pt/U plasma}, \text{MHD stability})
\end{equation}
This core contributes to $\Phi_{A,int}$ and acts as a reference state.

\subsection{Gradient Solver}
A gradient solver computes $\nabla \Phi_{A}$ using magnetohydrodynamic
equations, Coulomb-barrier modulation for $Z = 76$--$92$, and atomic
flux--velocity coupling. The result is a field that can, in principle, guide
drift.

\subsection{Entropy Reinforcement Engine}
Fusion energy spikes are converted into atomic-scale entropy:
\begin{equation}
    S_{A} = k_{S} \cdot E_{spike}
\end{equation}
Entropy reinforcement deepens the forward portion of the atomic gradient,
increasing drift potential.

\subsection{Atomic Drift Drive}
The atomic drift drive couples the craft's structure to $\nabla \Phi_{A}$.
Motion arises as passive drift along the gradient rather than active thrust.

\section{Effective Motion Law}
The effective drift velocity of FAE is modeled as:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff} \propto k_{A} \cdot \left|\nabla \Phi_{A}\right| \cdot S_{A}
\end{equation}
where $k_{A}$ is an atomic coupling coefficient, $\left|\nabla \Phi_{A}\right|$
is the magnitude of the atomic stability gradient, and $S_{A}$ is the entropy
reinforcement term.

A fundamental constraint is imposed:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff} < c
\end{equation}
FAE is therefore positioned as a relativistic propulsion concept that
asymptotically approaches, but does not exceed, the speed of light.

\section{Performance Envelope}
Conceptual performance bands can be defined in terms of gradient strength:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Band I (Shallow): Low $\left|\nabla \Phi_{A}\right|$, micro-drift.
    \item Band II (Moderate): Controlled drift, medium velocities.
    \item Band III (Strong): High-velocity drift, pre-relativistic regimes.
    \item Band IV (Extreme): Relativistic drift, near-$c$ behavior.
\end{itemize}
These bands are qualitative and serve as a framework for future theoretical
and simulation studies.

\section{Discussion}
The First Atoms Engine is not currently engineerable with existing technology.
Its modules---atomic state control, fusion anchors, and macroscopic atomic
gradients---require advances in nuclear physics, field control, and materials.
However, FAE provides a structured conceptual model for atomic-gradient
propulsion and suggests a research direction focused on stability-biased motion
rather than force-based thrust.

\section{Conclusion}
FAE defines an atomic-gradient propulsion architecture in which motion emerges
from engineered energy-density differences at the atomic scale. By coupling
internal fusion anchors to external stability fields and reinforcing gradients
with entropy, FAE offers a conceptual path toward continuous, relativistic
drift bounded by the speed of light. This work serves as a theoretical white
sheet for future exploration of atomic-state propulsion.

